<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><channel><language>en</language><title>International news from the Americas - France 24</title><description>FRANCE 24’s coverage of the political, social and economic issues in the United States, Canada, Mexico, Argentina, Brazil and across the Americas.</description><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/</link><lastBuildDate>Thu, 29 Oct 2026 02:54:37 GMT</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://www.france24.com/en/americas/rss" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><category>A propos</category><title>Flavio Bolsonaro claims he is Brazil's true leader after fleeing to US</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/a-propos/20261028-flavio-bolsonaro-claims-he-is-brazil-s-true-leader-after-fleeing-to-us</link><description>
Brazil's defeated right-wing presidential candidate Flavio Bolsonaro insisted Wednesday he is his country's rightful leader after arriving in the United States to start a self-imposed exile. Bolsonaro's defiance on fleeing to Florida signaled continued upheaval for Brazil, where leftist President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva won a dramatic runoff election Sunday. FRANCE 24's Sharon Gaffney speaks with Fabio de Sa e Silva, Associate Professor of International Studies at the University of Oklahoma. He says that an attempt by Bolsonaro to turn a domestic dispute into a wider international issue "will not be successful", explaining that he is likely going to be "losing the grip of the opposition, there is a vacuum now that others will seek to occupy" and that it's unlikely that the United States will "get involved into questioning the results of the Brazilian election".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2a0a87d6-d31a-11f1-a45a-811458fcf026/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261028-223115-224345-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2a0a87d6-d31a-11f1-a45a-811458fcf026/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261028-223115-224345-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">068c4a94-d31d-11f1-8572-d950a4a98ba5</guid><pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 22:15:03 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Sharon GAFFNEY</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Flavio Bolsonaro claims he is Brazil's true leader after fleeing to US</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/flavio-bolsonaro-claims-he-is-brazil-s-true-leader-after-fleeing-to-us</link><description>
Brazil's defeated right-wing presidential candidate Flavio Bolsonaro insisted Wednesday he is his country's rightful leader after arriving in the United States to start a self-imposed exile. Bolsonaro's defiance on fleeing to Florida signaled continued upheaval for Brazil, where leftist President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva won a dramatic runoff election Sunday. FRANCE 24's Sharon Gaffney speaks with Fabio de Sa e Silva, Associate Professor of International Studies at the University of Oklahoma. He says that an attempt by Bolsonaro to turn a domestic dispute into a wider international issue "will not be successful", explaining that he is likely going to be "losing the grip of the opposition, there is a vacuum now that others will seek to occupy" and that it's unlikely that the United States will "get involved into questioning the results of the Brazilian election".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2a0a87d6-d31a-11f1-a45a-811458fcf026/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261028-223115-224345-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2a0a87d6-d31a-11f1-a45a-811458fcf026/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261028-223115-224345-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">2f61c67c-d31a-11f1-b491-9f80094824d5</guid><pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 22:13:27 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Top US federal judge who handled 9/11, Trump, Maduro cases dies aged 92</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261028-top-us-federal-judge-who-handled-9-11-trump-maduro-cases-dies-aged-92</link><description>
Alvin Hellerstein, a top US judge who was handling the trial against deposed Venezuelan leader Nicholas Maduro and previously oversaw lawsuits stemming from the September 11 attacks on the World Trade Centre and multiple cases involving President Donald Trump, died on Wednesday aged 92.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/03fc7ce6-d2fe-11f1-a7b8-efcc5769f1db/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-08T171620Z-1636244633-RC25ZNAVWNT2-RTRMADP-3-USA-VENEZUELA-MADURO-FLORES.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/03fc7ce6-d2fe-11f1-a7b8-efcc5769f1db/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-08T171620Z-1636244633-RC25ZNAVWNT2-RTRMADP-3-USA-VENEZUELA-MADURO-FLORES.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">72b3a0e4-d306-11f1-94c2-f1a9fd5afb11</guid><pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 20:22:05 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/03fc7ce6-d2fe-11f1-a7b8-efcc5769f1db/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-08T171620Z-1636244633-RC25ZNAVWNT2-RTRMADP-3-USA-VENEZUELA-MADURO-FLORES.jpg">© Jane Rosenberg, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Bolsonaro's escape to the US: How it happened</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/bolsonaro-s-escape-to-the-us-how-it-happened</link><description>
Brazil's defeated right-wing presidential candidate Flavio Bolsonaro arrived in Florida by private plane Wednesday after fleeing his country in defiance of a court order to surrender his passport. Bolsonaro, who lost a runoff election to incumbent Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva on Sunday, is under investigation in Brazil for alleged corruption and money laundering and, with his defeat, is set to lose parliamentary immunity. FRANCE 24's international affairs editor Kethevane Gorjestani explains.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/466b4824-d2e9-11f1-84e4-a7bbbeea0b20/w:1024/p:16x9/FlavioFloride.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/466b4824-d2e9-11f1-84e4-a7bbbeea0b20/w:1024/p:16x9/FlavioFloride.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">d7488292-d309-11f1-bcaf-c7c89510ff0a</guid><pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 20:06:05 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Bolsonaro flees Brazil for US after election defeat</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/bolsonaro-flees-brazil-for-us-after-election-defeat</link><description>
Flavio Bolsonaro's clandestine escape to the United States bewildered Brazil and triggered a frantic search for his whereabouts. The Supreme Court had ordered the son of jailed ex-leader Jair Bolsonaro to forfeit his passports after his electoral loss on Sunday, amid fears he would leave the country due to ongoing investigations against him. But the defeated right-wing presidential candidate failed to hand them in, choosing instead to flee to the US via Argentina and Ecuador. FRANCE 24's Tim Vickery reports from Rio de Janeiro.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5551a2de-d307-11f1-ab35-75c7729144f1/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261028-201254-202039-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/5551a2de-d307-11f1-ab35-75c7729144f1/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261028-201254-202039-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">5a9513fc-d307-11f1-aa36-21d97535601f</guid><pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 19:44:17 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>'Si, se puede': US civil rights icon Dolores Huerta dies at 96</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261028-si-se-puede-us-civil-rights-icon-dolores-huerta-dies-96</link><description>
Influential US labour union co-founder, feminist, and civil rights icon Dolores Huerta died on Wednesday in Bakersfield, California, her family said. Credited with coining the enduring "Si, se puede!" rallying cry later adapted by former US president Barack Obama, Huerta continued her activism on race, poverty, LGBTQ and women's rights issues until the last months of her life. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/aafa5844-d2f7-11f1-a891-75c7729144f1/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26077703606889.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/aafa5844-d2f7-11f1-a891-75c7729144f1/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26077703606889.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">d7407b18-d2f2-11f1-935c-e70d575d75fb</guid><pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 17:49:46 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/aafa5844-d2f7-11f1-a891-75c7729144f1/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26077703606889.jpg">© Damian Dovarganes, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>US grants visa to defeated Brazilian presidential candidate Flavio Bolsonaro</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-us-grants-visa-to-defeated-brazilian-presidential-candidate-flavio-bolsonaro</link><description>
Brazil's defeated presidential candidate Flavio Bolsonaro and his immediate family landed in Miami, Florida on Wednesday after he refused to surrender his passports as ordered by his country's Supreme Court in connection with a money-laundering probe. The US State Department on Tuesday announced it had granted entry visas to the senator and his family. France 24's Kethevane Gorjestani explains. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/88c25a40-d2ef-11f1-a32e-47de50b3c1e4/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-25dcf831.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/88c25a40-d2ef-11f1-a32e-47de50b3c1e4/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-25dcf831.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8db11448-d2ee-11f1-b989-a7b82ac76b2c</guid><pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 17:00:58 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Defeated presidential candidate Bolsonaro lands in Miami after fleeing Brazil</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-defeated-presidential-candidate-bolsonaro-lands-in-miami-after-fleeing-brazil</link><description>
Brazilian Senator ​Flavio Bolsonaro and his family arrived in Miami on Wednesday, where the United States government granted them ​entry visas after he refused to comply with a Brazilian Supreme Court order to surrender his passports. Bolsonaro lost Sunday's presidential election to leftist incumbent President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/27e463cc-d2ee-11f1-b552-a7bbbeea0b20/w:1024/p:16x9/minibol.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/27e463cc-d2ee-11f1-b552-a7bbbeea0b20/w:1024/p:16x9/minibol.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">a7b683a0-d2ee-11f1-bc73-0ff6e8c66d17</guid><pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 16:50:18 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Defeated Brazilian presidential candidate Flavio Bolsonaro lands in Miami</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-defeated-brazilian-presidential-candidate-flavio-bolsonaro-lands-in-miami</link><description>
Defeated Brazilian presidential candidate Flavio Bolsonaro and his family landed in Miami on Wednesday (October 28) after fleeing Brazil. The stunning move came after 48 hours in which Bolsonaro refused to concede to Brazilian President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva and then threatened to attempt to annul the election result. Details by FRANCE 24's correspondent Tim Vickery.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/8afb5d34-d2e5-11f1-98a1-75c7729144f1/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26301497988107-1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/8afb5d34-d2e5-11f1-98a1-75c7729144f1/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26301497988107-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1332caa4-d2e4-11f1-8de1-d5fbe47d5eb8</guid><pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 16:28:38 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Brazil: Flavio Bolsonaro leaves Argentina for alleged US asylum after defeat</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-brazil-flavio-bolsonaro-leaves-argentina-for-alleged-us-asylum-after-defeat</link><description>
Flavio Bolsonaro, who lost Brazil's runoff election to incumbent Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva on Sunday, boarded a flight to Ecuador after arriving in Argentina on Tuesday night, October 27. He is expected to land in Miami, as the US State Department said it would issue visas to him and his family. France 24's correspondent in Rio de Janeiro Tim Vickery reports the latest. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/dbc8ed9c-d2bf-11f1-9e7c-a7bbbeea0b20/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-c1a689f8.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/dbc8ed9c-d2bf-11f1-9e7c-a7bbbeea0b20/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-c1a689f8.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">ba10c5d2-d2bd-11f1-b540-8d64737128b3</guid><pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 11:43:38 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Brazil: Bolsonaro defeated and on the run</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/brazil-bolsonaro-defeated-and-on-the-run</link><description>
Defeated far-right presidential candidate Flávio Bolsonaro has left Brazil for the United States, despite a court order barring him from leaving the country. The son of jailed former president Jair Bolsonaro has yet to concede defeat in Sunday’s election and is under investigation in Brazil for alleged corruption and money laundering. Antonia Kerrigan has more.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4fa02f68-d2bc-11f1-a74e-75c7729144f1/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261028-103955-104147-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4fa02f68-d2bc-11f1-a74e-75c7729144f1/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261028-103955-104147-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">55119e28-d2bc-11f1-ab34-8b4284101d7d</guid><pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 11:30:09 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>US midterms: Slashing of food stamps in Arizona angers voters</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-us-midterms-slashing-of-food-stamps-in-arizona-angers-voters</link><description>
With the US midterm elections just under a week away, the cost of living has emerged as a key campaign issue. In Arizona, many Americans are criticising the administration’s decision to slash food stamps, which has had significant consequences for the poorest. Camille Corcoran reports. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/52373586-d2b8-11f1-a2b6-a7bbbeea0b20/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-b39b422e.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/52373586-d2b8-11f1-a2b6-a7bbbeea0b20/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-b39b422e.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4a53ab68-d2b5-11f1-b336-055a9c4e66b3</guid><pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 10:43:14 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Flavio Bolsonaro on way to Ecuador from Argentina after fleeing Brazil</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-flavio-bolsonaro-on-way-to-ecuador-from-argentina-after-fleeing-brazil</link><description>
Defeated right-wing presidential candidate Flavio Bolsonaro was on his way to Ecuador after fleeing Brazil, Argentina's government said on Wednesday, October 28. The 45-year-old senator and his family were granted United States visas the day before by the Trump administration. France 24's correspondent in Washington Fraser Jackson reports the latest. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4bb548ba-d2b8-11f1-8e09-a7bbbeea0b20/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-26T022137Z-995936666-RC2PAOAXW1YT-RTRMADP-3-BRAZIL-ELECTION.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4bb548ba-d2b8-11f1-8e09-a7bbbeea0b20/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-26T022137Z-995936666-RC2PAOAXW1YT-RTRMADP-3-BRAZIL-ELECTION.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e2209696-d2b4-11f1-8fef-9dd4dab007b8</guid><pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 10:14:29 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Flavio Bolsonaro declares himself Brazil's 'true' leader after landing in Miami</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261028-brazil-s-flavio-bolsonaro-flies-on-from-ecuador-to-unknown-destination-despite-travel-ban</link><description>
Brazilian Senator Flavio said in a statement Wednesday that he was the "true president of Brazil", before revising his claim in a new statement and calling himself the country's "leader". Bolsonaro and his immediate family earlier landed in Miami, Florida after he refused to surrender his passports as ordered by his country's Supreme Court in connection with a money-laundering probe. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f0744206-d2a5-11f1-b348-efcc5769f1db/w:1024/p:16x9/61aaa17351d022a4e8e31524a80c92f74ff4fd2c.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f0744206-d2a5-11f1-b348-efcc5769f1db/w:1024/p:16x9/61aaa17351d022a4e8e31524a80c92f74ff4fd2c.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f10a8202-d2a5-11f1-87fc-078712dd45ca</guid><pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 08:02:37 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f0744206-d2a5-11f1-b348-efcc5769f1db/w:1024/p:16x9/61aaa17351d022a4e8e31524a80c92f74ff4fd2c.jpg">© Evaristo Sa, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Rights groups oppose UAE’s Lana Nusseibeh for UN chief over Sudan claims</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261028-lana-nusseibeh-un-chief-uae-sudan-rights-groups</link><description>
Human rights groups on Tuesday urged countries to reject UAE diplomat Lana Nusseibeh’s bid to become United Nations secretary general citing allegations that Abu Dhabi funds Sudan’s paramilitary Rapid Support Forces. The UAE denies the accusations. Nusseibeh, 48, is seeking to become the first woman to lead the UN.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ddd14ba6-d284-11f1-86b8-efcc5769f1db/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-27T223751Z-1217152200-RC2YBOA6HWDN-RTRMADP-3-UN-SECRETARYGENERAL-EMIRATES.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ddd14ba6-d284-11f1-86b8-efcc5769f1db/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-27T223751Z-1217152200-RC2YBOA6HWDN-RTRMADP-3-UN-SECRETARYGENERAL-EMIRATES.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f546a858-d27a-11f1-b65f-05b04e0a7656</guid><pubDate>Wed, 28 Oct 2026 02:56:59 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ddd14ba6-d284-11f1-86b8-efcc5769f1db/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-27T223751Z-1217152200-RC2YBOA6HWDN-RTRMADP-3-UN-SECRETARYGENERAL-EMIRATES.jpg">© Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>King Charles confronts calls for slavery apology during Caribbean tour</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261027-king-charles-slavery-apology-caribbean-tour</link><description>
King Charles III began a Caribbean tour in the Bahamas on Tuesday, acknowledging “the great suffering of the past” amid calls for an apology over slavery. The 77-year-old monarch, who is undergoing cancer treatment, will also visit Guyana and Antigua and Barbuda, highlighting climate threats and attending a Commonwealth summit with Queen Camilla.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e2ce5e36-d1cc-11f1-a1b1-5b4a75578ebb/w:1024/p:16x9/a76378f2ae9959050b5482d797377dd5c2291e0f.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e2ce5e36-d1cc-11f1-a1b1-5b4a75578ebb/w:1024/p:16x9/a76378f2ae9959050b5482d797377dd5c2291e0f.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">45cc8ee8-d25b-11f1-9fd9-273a6902d26e</guid><pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 23:14:34 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/e2ce5e36-d1cc-11f1-a1b1-5b4a75578ebb/w:1024/p:16x9/a76378f2ae9959050b5482d797377dd5c2291e0f.jpg">© Aaron Chown, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Brazil's Flavio Bolsonaro leaves Argentina for alleged US asylum after defeat</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261027-defeated-brazilian-candidate-flavio-bolsonaro-passports-cancelled-whereabouts-unknown</link><description>
Brazilian former presidential candidate Flávio Bolsonaro travelled to Argentina before flying to Ecuador, Argentina's government said Wednesday, days after his election defeat to President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva. He is en route to the US where allegedly he will seek asylum following his passports being nullified after failure to comply with a Supreme Court order in a money-laundering probe.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/6a7c52da-d221-11f1-9049-75c7729144f1/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26299067954788.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/6a7c52da-d221-11f1-9049-75c7729144f1/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26299067954788.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">95b0224c-d221-11f1-864e-f1256c5a284c</guid><pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 16:33:13 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/6a7c52da-d221-11f1-9049-75c7729144f1/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26299067954788.jpg">© Eraldo Peres, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>USA: Could a Democrat win a statewide election in Texas?</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261027-usa-could-a-democrat-win-a-statewide-election-in-texas</link><description>
There is only one week left until the US midterm elections. In Texas, Democratic candidate James Talarico, could win a Senate seat for the first time in decades.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1c073ffe-d215-11f1-91ad-5b4a75578ebb/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26299284061928.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1c073ffe-d215-11f1-91ad-5b4a75578ebb/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26299284061928.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">71ea8724-d214-11f1-a70b-95616215da57</guid><pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 15:47:42 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>'Get the Flock out': Americans respond to AI cameras in bipartisan backlash</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261027-united-states-get-the-flock-out-americans-respond-to-ai-cameras-in-bipartisan-backlash-midterm-elections</link><description>
